{
 "result": "wf.py is built and verified: it reads text on stdin and prints the 10 most common lowercase words as 'word count', ties broken by word, with words defined as maximal runs of ASCII letters. It execs a compiled C helper, wf_c (built from wf.c: one read of stdin, in-place lowercasing, an open-addressing FNV hash table, then qsort), compiling it on demand and falling back to a pure-Python Counter version if the binary is missing and cannot be built. On the 21 MB generated big.txt its output is byte-identical to expected.txt, and so is that of the independent regex/Counter reference ref.py (WF_MATCH and REF_MATCH on the final body). A random differential test against the reference found 0 mismatches, and empty input exits 0 with no output. The assay scores 1 / (minimum CPU seconds, user+sys, over 5 runs), not wall time, because wall time varied more than 15% here. The final body scores 9.79 and 9.80 (about 0.10 s CPU including Python startup; earlier runs ranged 8.26 to 9.89), against the registered baseline of 2.434 for the pure-Python version, roughly 4x. The one `try` (an mmap/uint64-key rewrite of the helper) produced no gain because `cc` failed in the daughter's sandbox, so that idea was never measured.",
